From: Win7 port restore
Subject: [PATCH] desktop/index.html: container-query fallback for the H5 host/IP row

The H5 access card lays the host/IP row out as
sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_9rem_9rem] — a *viewport* breakpoint. On the
settings page the row sits inside nested cards whose combined chrome
eats most of the window, so even with the viewport well past `sm` the
grid box itself can be too narrow for 1fr + 9rem + 9rem, the flexible
column collapses, and the host/IP input only shows its full width when
the window is maximized.

Give the row's parent wrapper an inline-size containment context (the
div one level above the grid — a container query cannot target its own
container) and drop the grid to a single minmax(0,1fr) column when the
*container*, not the viewport, is narrower than 28rem. Container
queries and :has() both ship in Chromium 105+, so Electron 22's
Chromium 108 handles this natively. The :has() shapes are stable
structural anchors: the wrapper is the only div whose div > div chain
ends at #h5-access-public-url, and the grid is its direct child.

Same tag id and rules as the in-place hotfix baked into the shipped
Offline installer's dist/index.html (<style id="h5-host-input-fix">).

diff --git a/desktop/index.html b/desktop/index.html
index f516f8b..21e889c 100644
--- a/desktop/index.html
+++ b/desktop/index.html
@@ -720,6 +720,7 @@
         })
       })()
     </script>
+    <style id="h5-host-input-fix">div:has(> div > div > #h5-access-public-url){container-type:inline-size}@container (max-width: 28rem){div:has(> div > #h5-access-public-url){grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)!important}}</style>
   </head>
   <body>
     <div id="root"></div>
